#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228902 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "533cdb5e-1d90-5aae-961b-4fe7daa30b0c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6617" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+y=\\tan{x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x + g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan x \\sin x\\,\\textrm{d}x\n= -\\sin x + \\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x)=-\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x)=\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=\\tan x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=\\cos x,\n\\qquad\ny_2(x)=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|)+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\,{{-\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\cos x\\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228903 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Real Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "d7e995ec-7c1a-510d-be52-5ef3ce5f77aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-6618" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny'' - 2y' + y = 18x e^{x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x} + g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ may be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x) = -\\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x) = \\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})-({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}+x{e}^{2x}-x{e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nNow compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot x{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-18\\int {x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =18\\int x\\,dx \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-6{x}^{3}){e}^{x}+(9{x}^{2})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3}{e}^{x}+9{x}^{3}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}+{{3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x) = 3x^3 e^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb69a321-2b4b-59e4-91a1-294654418129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3x}(x^{2}\\arctan x + 2\\ln (1 + x^{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228387} {:answer/id #uuid "ce59769f-d45f-52f4-a21f-948a616ccbfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3x}(2x\\arctan x - \\ln (1 + x^{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228388} {:answer/id #uuid "d49ed170-d1a3-5a8f-802c-adf79dbf8451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3x}(x^{2}\\arctan x + 4\\ln (1 + x^{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228389} {:answer/id #uuid "1922057e-9c1b-593f-84d6-0c5c14e6eca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3x}(x\\arctan x + 4\\ln (1 + x^{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228390} {:answer/id #uuid "7d648f51-f6f2-5f4f-849c-040d10ff79ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3x}(x^{2}\\arctan x + \\ln (1 + x^{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce59769f-d45f-52f4-a21f-948a616ccbfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3x}(2x\\arctan x - \\ln (1 + x^{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228388} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9261647-99e3-5267-a388-8b64afbe051c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12871450-b62c-5f51-815b-c8231596a048" :question/math-academy-id "q-337391" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y=\\frac{2{e}^{3x}}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(x{e}^{3x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}(x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x})-(3{e}^{3x})(x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}+3x{e}^{6x}-3x{e}^{6x} \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{2{e}^{3x}\\cdot x{e}^{3x}}{(1+{x}^{2}){e}^{6x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}) \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{2{e}^{3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}}{(1+{x}^{2}){e}^{6x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we needed to use substitution $t=1+{x}^{2}$ to find ${u}_{1}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))+(x{e}^{3x})(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}+{{{e}^{3x}(2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)={e}^{3x}(2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371596 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8774dfe6-cbe4-57fb-86ab-ce8b60c2e599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{5}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371598} {:answer/id #uuid "7474064a-cb39-5f0a-9caf-75adbcdb96bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371599} {:answer/id #uuid "69c42a36-92cb-5c22-a1cc-c59b419b1499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{3}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371600} {:answer/id #uuid "520cd96c-5614-53aa-94c8-f37070a8c7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{3x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371601} {:answer/id #uuid "8edcf2f4-928e-56a3-9b02-60b50bcc082b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7474064a-cb39-5f0a-9caf-75adbcdb96bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371599} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4a84989-00c3-5ac9-9165-ba409c0d8565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6c167fc-51f7-5156-879d-32c227a0d2b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-337541" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-12y=14x{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int x{e}^{-7x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=14x{e}^{-4x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})({e}^{3x})^{′}-({e}^{-4x})^{′}({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})(3{e}^{3x})-(-4{e}^{-4x})({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-x}+4{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =7{e}^{-x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{14x{e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}}{7{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{14x{e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{-4x}}{7{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x{e}^{-7x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2})({e}^{-4x})+[-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}]({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2x}{7}-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+(-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2x}{7}-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =(A-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $A$ as\n\n$$\nA:=(A-\\frac{2}{49})\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}{{-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "803505c1-22fa-5e6a-ad9c-b4a70555a4cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{3}+2x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371605} {:answer/id #uuid "70b48aab-4042-5c20-ade9-5665d9c9c5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(2{x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371606} {:answer/id #uuid "ede90880-a39e-51e4-afb6-27741eaf96b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(2{x}^{2}+4x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371607} {:answer/id #uuid "66298ada-d1cf-5925-8d6c-443d5d2e8f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371608} {:answer/id #uuid "3f970cb6-ba77-55eb-a1c1-a7fa84216e25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+4x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66298ada-d1cf-5925-8d6c-443d5d2e8f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371608} :answer-field/id #uuid "74379a5f-fc24-5ff9-b492-4cab6807d2e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28a99110-2e19-556d-8656-9d4cd7895e05" :question/math-academy-id "q-337542" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+6y=2x{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int x{e}^{-x}dx=-(x+1){e}^{-x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x{e}^{2x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})({e}^{3x})^{′}-({e}^{2x})^{′}({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(3{e}^{3x})-(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{5x}-2{e}^{5x} \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{2x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}}{{e}^{5x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{2x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}}{{e}^{5x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x{e}^{-x}\\text{ }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2})({e}^{2x})+[-2(x+1){e}^{-x}({e}^{3x})] \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}-2(x+1){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+2){e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}-({x}^{2}+2x+2){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =(A-2){e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}-({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $A$ as\n\n$$\nA:=(A-2)\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}\\,{{-\\,({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc68a939-1bc8-5d5a-b2af-5ddf895e38d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371612} {:answer/id #uuid "59673c0f-1dc1-5444-bb62-100eaac73c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371613} {:answer/id #uuid "57528d90-9d29-59c4-bd1a-011b766fa35b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371614} {:answer/id #uuid "08d29d06-7b60-5b53-8826-b51924725406" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371615} {:answer/id #uuid "b6fd716d-4be4-57e3-ba16-9a2b4f190b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57528d90-9d29-59c4-bd1a-011b766fa35b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371614} :answer-field/id #uuid "18ed1ff4-eccd-5722-b753-253b967edebb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06701a13-3cbb-58f0-8fe6-88db9f0d26c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-337547" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+4y=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(x{e}^{2x})^{′}-({e}^{2x})^{′}(x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x})-(2{e}^{2x})(x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}+2x{e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}\\cdot x{e}^{2x}}{{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}}{{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|)({e}^{2x})+(-\\frac{1}{x})(x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+1) \\\\  & =(A-1){e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $A$ as\n\n$$\nA:=(A-1)\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}{{\\,-\\,{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "128a80d7-7962-53e6-a915-c0efb833a234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371619} {:answer/id #uuid "2589c8fe-af19-59fd-a747-36c7a120e107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371620} {:answer/id #uuid "f7580637-c1de-5426-8cb5-844da030df7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371621} {:answer/id #uuid "18848514-50d8-5bc9-8226-5c35e03f3e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371622} {:answer/id #uuid "64f1e5c2-cf7e-54a2-a869-6d7a8e4ca2e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18848514-50d8-5bc9-8226-5c35e03f3e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371622} :answer-field/id #uuid "85982ca7-fc0e-5099-b92e-f7ca3c9ae60e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "000d5951-3272-57cb-a70c-1551b6af9160" :question/math-academy-id "q-337548" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(x{e}^{-x})^{′}-({e}^{-x})^{′}(x{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x})-(-{e}^{-x})(x{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}-x{e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{-2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\cdot x{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{3}{e}^{-2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\cdot {e}^{-x}}{{x}^{3}{e}^{-2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}){e}^{-x}+(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})x{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{x}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+{{\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229665 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a14792f2-5973-59c5-9c53-4108312545d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\sin 2x + \\cos 2x\\ln \\mid \\cos x \\mid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228409} {:answer/id #uuid "ac229b5c-ac37-58e8-a2a7-8f3098c4869c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin 2x\\ln \\mid \\cos x \\mid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228410} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a6fd65-79da-5c8f-822d-7346d7fb32cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\cos 2x + \\sin 2x\\ln \\mid \\cos x \\mid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228411} {:answer/id #uuid "e44f7165-d39a-58b9-ae7f-9d82e8ba2e27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\cos 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228412} {:answer/id #uuid "fc169e50-ea5d-5ece-8615-5fd5867208cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\cos 2x + \\sin 2x\\ln \\mid \\cos x \\mid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc169e50-ea5d-5ece-8615-5fd5867208cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\cos 2x + \\sin 2x\\ln \\mid \\cos x \\mid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228413} :answer-field/id #uuid "874bf077-7f9c-52f1-8b97-fdb53377c843" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00fa4d0d-a8d6-536d-9f44-d4c9796c3f4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337950" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following results (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡2x+2{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(B-\\frac{1}{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $B$ as\n\n$$\nB:=(B-\\frac{1}{2})\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,{{-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93a524bc-dbe0-527a-9025-3d60ee66662c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369114} {:answer/id #uuid "4f701a29-414c-5242-af93-500e7082a757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369115} {:answer/id #uuid "2263fea8-99ab-5528-9114-c1a5f4d13e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369116} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbfd963-2906-5722-b152-e3c6726d4626" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369117} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3d0790-793b-5370-982b-173ea365929b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2263fea8-99ab-5528-9114-c1a5f4d13e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369116} :answer-field/id #uuid "210210bc-cd82-5454-b1dc-84e092e994aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6d64847-1769-5602-97fa-762ad6861828" :question/math-academy-id "q-338142" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+5y=4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,dx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{2x}{cos}^{2}⁡2x-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{2x}{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}({cos}^{2}⁡2x+{sin}^{2}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-2\\int dx \\\\  & =-2x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd9029a9-fbd0-500a-a64e-34192a9548dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371626} {:answer/id #uuid "d72a261d-8fcd-594f-a08b-c2895688f990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371627} {:answer/id #uuid "227c5b6f-0c99-58f0-a784-abc08f190577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371628} {:answer/id #uuid "80b444f0-2564-5163-aa4f-e5e8bb5c1ef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371629} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6471cc-b71a-5817-8290-097d974bcf9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80b444f0-2564-5163-aa4f-e5e8bb5c1ef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371629} :answer-field/id #uuid "cffb04b3-0202-53e2-9cac-2592580f6980" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "320207c2-9021-5944-819c-2e3cc83910e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-338528" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y={csc}^{3}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x=2{cos}^{2}⁡2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={csc}^{3}⁡2x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡2x+2{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{csc}^{3}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {csc}^{2}⁡2x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{csc}^{3}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{{sin}^{3}⁡2x}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Then $du=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,dx,$ so $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,dx=\\frac{1}{2}\\,du,$ and the integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{{sin}^{3}⁡2x}\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,du) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{-3}\\,du \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}{u}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8{sin}^{2}⁡2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+(-\\frac{1}{8{sin}^{2}⁡2x})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2x}{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}-\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}(2{cos}^{2}⁡2x-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,+\\,{{\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94d95791-2cc7-5a8b-962f-73c236a5c448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369121} {:answer/id #uuid "de4cbfee-dc26-5f53-9de8-a54d1fed0a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369122} {:answer/id #uuid "bc64cc80-c370-594d-8ddb-00cdb5c8bcb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369123} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbc4f3e-ef1a-51f8-837a-6b1ed0325ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369124} {:answer/id #uuid "c11e43fe-ba7a-53c0-90d7-a59aa3b96b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bbc4f3e-ef1a-51f8-837a-6b1ed0325ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369124} :answer-field/id #uuid "01fdd2a3-162b-5f4d-bd71-bfbd4cb30ce6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb7b8734-1edc-5c36-87f9-ef5befb2dd32" :question/math-academy-id "q-338767" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+5y=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following results (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=x{e}^{2x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{e}^{4x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{e}^{4x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,(x{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =x{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{{x{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "591936b0-73c0-5cbe-9c61-86106b4f2f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371633} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc4595e-549c-561b-8030-1233c4b6d192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371634} {:answer/id #uuid "6860da19-ba72-51a4-9aef-ec2a8bc8e60e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371635} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed31006-e747-5a19-93dc-7c3d3751e4f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371636} {:answer/id #uuid "3f84b1b9-c4f7-556c-9f6a-464cba731885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f84b1b9-c4f7-556c-9f6a-464cba731885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371637} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d485d7d-8d0f-51cf-9ee0-47ed5776a788" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fb5263d-b905-5758-8aab-2336a62acfc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-338774" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{e}^{-2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{e}^{-2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int dx \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|)({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(x{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$"}]}
